; Tank-AI Phase 1 CBOR record schema, version 1.
;
; ADR-0004 decision 6: records are CBOR, described by this CDDL schema.
; ADR-0004 decision 9: records identify signals by numeric id and carry one
;   timestamp per scan.
; ADR-0004 decision 11: time is SNTP where available, otherwise boot-relative,
;   with a boot id and a "time not yet known" flag.
; ADR-0008: every value carries a quality flag and an age.
;
; Version 1 gained the gap record in S-040 and the batch in S-062 before any
; device or cloud consumed the schema, so `v` stays 1.
;
; The device is send-only (ADR-0004 decision 10). There is no command record
; and no inbound message type in Phase 1; adding one would be a phase change.
;
; Map keys are short text strings rather than integers: the record is a
; cross-team contract that four components read, and the compact-on-the-wire
; job is done by the delta-encoded flash log (ADR-0004 decision 12) and by
; sending scaled integers instead of floats.

; A device sends scan records, and a gap record when its outage buffer had to
; drop scans that were never delivered (S-040). The two are told apart by
; their keys: a scan record has `r`, a gap record has `gap`.
;
; On the wire a device sends a batch (S-062; tech lead, 26 Nov 2026): one to
; five records, oldest first, in one FRDM-to-ESP32 link frame and one MQTT
; message on tank/{device_id}/records, normally five one-second records every
; 5 s. Each record in a batch is complete on its own, `v` and `dev` included,
; so a consumer can split a batch and handle the records one at a time; a
; single message is therefore valid by itself too.
start = batch / message
batch = [1*5 message]
message = record / gap-record

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A scan record: one sample of a set of signals, from one device, at one time.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
record = {
  v: 1,                   ; this schema version; bump for an incompatible change
  dev: device-id,
  sl: uint,               ; signals-v?.yaml `version` the ids below come from
  boot: boot-id,
  seq: uint,              ; per-boot record counter, for delivery confirmation
  up: uint,               ; milliseconds since boot, always present
  tk: bool,               ; true when wall-clock time is known
  ? t: uint,              ; Unix seconds UTC; present only when tk is true
  r: [+ reading],
}

; The device id is the identifier in the commissioning QR code (ADR-0005). It
; is not a customer, plant or tank name: the cloud holds that mapping, assigned
; once when an admin claims the device.
device-id = tstr .size (1..64)

; Random per-boot identifier, so records buffered before the clock was known
; can be placed on a timeline once it is (ADR-0004 decision 11).
boot-id = bstr .size 16

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A gap record: scans that were measured but will never arrive (S-040).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ADR-0004 decision 12: when the flash log is full the oldest data is dropped
; and a gap is recorded. Without this record the cloud would see only a
; silent hole, which a trend would show as "nothing happened" (ADR-0008).
; Every scan from `first` to `last`, both included, was dropped; they may
; come from different boots. `lost` is how many dropped scans this record
; counts: each dropped scan is counted in exactly one gap record, but the
; ranges of two gap records may overlap. The cloud must not interpolate
; across a gap.
gap-record = {
  v: 1,
  dev: device-id,
  gap: {
    lost: uint,           ; dropped scans counted here, at least 1
    first: lost-scan,     ; the oldest scan dropped
    last: lost-scan,      ; the newest scan dropped
  },
}

; Where a dropped scan sat in time: the same time fields as its scan record.
lost-scan = {
  boot: boot-id,
  seq: uint,
  up: uint,
  tk: bool,
  ? t: uint,              ; present only when tk is true
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One signal's value.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; `val` is a scaled integer or a bool, never a float: multiply by the signal's
; `scale` from the signal list to get the engineering value. A reading whose
; `q` is not `good` must not be shown as a live measurement (AGENTS.md section
; 7); a failed sensor is flagged and excluded, never silently replaced, so
; `val` is absent when there is no trustworthy number to send.
reading = {
  id: uint,               ; `id` from the signal list
  ? val: int / bool,      ; absent when q is fault or unconfigured
  q: quality,
  age: uint,              ; milliseconds between sampling this signal and `up`
}

quality = &quality-values
quality-values = (
  good: 0,                ; sampled normally, inside the valid range
  stale: 1,               ; last known value, not refreshed this scan
  out-of-range: 2,        ; sampled, but outside the signal's valid_range
  fault: 3,               ; sensor or bus failure; no value is sent
  unconfigured: 4,        ; channel present in hardware but not commissioned
)
